s on the intersection of technology and social justice. The symposium's program, available online, lists Eliana as an affiliated researcher with the university's Department of Social Sciences. Another crucial piece of evidence lies in Eliana's own words. In an interview with a prominent Brazilian academic journal, she spoke candidly about the role the Federal University of Bahia played in shaping her research agenda. \"My time at the university was instrumental in helping me develop a nuanced understanding of the complex social dynamics at play in Bahia,\" she reflected. \"The support and guidance I received from my colleagues and mentors were invaluable in shaping my research trajectory.\""}, {"doc_id": "d2", "text": "As the sun set over the ancient city of Isfahan, casting a warm orange glow over the intricate tilework of the Imam Square, Dr. Eliana Amorim de Souza made her way through the bustling streets, her worn leather satchel slung over her shoulder. She had grown accustomed to the rhythms of this city, where the call to prayer from the Masoumeh Mosque mingled with the chatter of students and vendors. It was a place where tradition and innovation coexisted, much like the fusion of ancient Persian architecture and modern medical facilities that defined Isfahan University of Medical Sciences. Eliana, a renowned researcher in her field, had been drawn to the university's esteemed faculty of medicine, where she had spent the past three years collaborating with colleagues on groundbreaking projects. Her ORCID ID, 0000-0002-9653-3164, was a testament to her prolific output, with publications in top-tier journals and presentations at conferences around the world. As she approached the university's main campus, Eliana exchanged warm greetings with the familiar faces of her colleagues, who were gathered outside the faculty building, sipping tea and discussing the latest breakthroughs in medical research. Dr. Mohammad Rezaei, a soft-spoken expert in epidemiology, beck